content.thinking:
The user reports two bugs:

1. **后端运行期间执行 npm run seed 会静默回滚重置结果** — When running `npm run seed` while the backend is running, the seed results are silently rolled back. 

Why? Because sql.js is an in-memory database per process. The seed script runs in a separate process, writes to mro.db file. But the running server has its own in-memory copy. When the server next persists (any write operation), it exports its in-memory DB and overwrites mro.db — silently clobbering the seed results. So the seed appears to work (file written), but as soon as the server handles any write request, the file gets overwritten with the server's stale in-memory state. The seed is "silently rolled back."

Fix options:
- Make the server detect that the DB file changed on disk and reload. E.g., watch the file (fs.watch) or check mtime before each request/operation and reload if changed externally.
- Better: track a "last known mtime" — on each persist, check if the file's mtime is newer than our last write; if so, reload from disk first (external change detected). Or simply: before each write persist, and also reload when file changes.

A clean approach: in `_persist()`, before writing, check if the file was modified externally (mtimeMs != our last recorded mtime). If externally modified, we could reload instead of overwrite. But mid-transaction state would be lost... Actually simpler and more robust for this demo: add `fs.watchFile` / `fs.watch` on the DB file; when it changes and the change wasn't caused by our own write, reload the database from disk.

Let me design:

```js
let lastWriteToken = 0; // or track mtime we last wrote

_persist() {
  if (this._txnDepth > 0) return;
  fs.writeFileSync(DB_PATH, Buffer.from(this._db.export()));
  this._lastMtime = fs.statSync(DB_PATH).mtimeMs;
}
```

And a watcher:

```js
fs.watch(DB_PATH, (event) => {
  // ignore our own writes
  const mtime = fs.statSync(DB_PATH).mtimeMs;
  if (mtime === this._lastMtime) return; // hmm, mtime resolution
  ...
});
```

fs.watch can fire multiple times and mtime comparison is racy. Alternative approach: compare file contents hash? For a demo DB this is small (a few hundred KB max), so hashing is cheap. On persist: compute hash of exported buffer, store it. Watcher: read file, hash it, if different from our last hash → external change → reload.

Reload procedure: `const bytes = fs.readFileSync(DB_PATH); this._db.close(); this._db = new SQL.Database(bytes);` — need to keep the SQL module reference around. Also re-apply `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON`.

But careful: `this._db` is referenced by Statement objects via `this.db._db` — they access `this.db._db` at call time (in run/get/all they do `this.db._db.prepare(...)`), so replacing `this._db` is fine since statements look it up lazily. Let me check my Statement class: `run(...params) { const stmt = this.db._db.prepare(this.sql); ...}` — yes, lazy lookup. Good.

